# Failures that are not evidence about a mutant.
#
# `scripts/mutate` judges a mutant killed when the suite goes red with it in place. That is sound
# only while every way the suite can go red is a way the mutant caused. It is not: a test that can
# fail because the machine was busy fails the same way whether a mutant is present or absent, and
# the harness cannot tell the two apart. PR #109 is the case this file exists for — R9 was reported
# KILLED by exactly one test, `asyncProcessRunnerCancelsRunningProcess`; re-run alone against a
# clean baseline, R9 SURVIVED. The mutant was never killed. Nothing in that report looked wrong,
# because a manufactured kill and a real one print the same line (SONNY-224).
#
# So the harness is told, here, which failures carry no information about a mutant. A failing test
# whose recorded issue matches a signature below is excluded from that mutant's kill count; a mutant
# with nothing else against it is reported UNATTRIBUTED rather than KILLED, and the battery exits 2
# the way a survivor does. Understating coverage is the safe direction. Manufacturing it is not.
#
# **Matched against the issue text, not the test name, and per issue rather than per test.** The
# same test can fail two ways: on a real assertion, which is evidence, and on a hang backstop, which
# is not. A name-based list would have to throw away both. Matching the message keeps the first and
# drops the second — a test is untrusted only when *every* issue it recorded is declared, and one
# undeclared issue makes it evidence again. It also needs no maintenance when a suite grows, because
# a new test written with the same helper produces the same message and is covered the day it lands.
#
# That per-issue property is stated here because the first version of the classifier did not have
# it — it ORed over a test's issues, so one declared backstop discarded a real assertion recorded
# beside it, which is exactly what this paragraph says a name list would do (PR #112 review, F2).
# Two limits are left, and both are the harness's rather than this file's. Two tests with the same
# function name in different suites merge, because swift-testing names no suite on either line the
# classifier reads; the merge undercounts and can misname, never the reverse. And a mutant whose
# only red is a *trapped* process names no test at all, so nothing here applies to it.
#
# Nothing in this file can excuse `UntrustedFailureDeclarationTests`. That suite is the guard on
# these declarations, so excusing its failure with the declarations it just called stale would be
# circular — `scripts/mutate` names those tests in `ALWAYS_TRUSTED` and treats them as evidence
# whatever they record.
#
# Format, one record per entry, directives at column 0. Comments and blank lines are ignored.
#
#   >>> signature   literal text, matched as a substring of the issue a failing test recorded.
#   >>> source      literal text that must still exist in one of the two test trees. This is what
#                   keeps the signature honest: `UntrustedFailureDeclarationTests` fails the suite
#                   when a `source` stops matching, which is what happens the moment someone rewords
#                   the helper a signature was written for. A signature that silently stops matching
#                   is the same defect as a listed test name that no longer exists — it stops
#                   protecting, and nothing says so.
#                   **Two trees, `Tests/` and `server/test/`** (SONNY-334). This harness has read
#                   vitest's log shapes as well as swift-testing's since SONNY-323 and matches a
#                   signature against the text a failure recorded without knowing what produced it,
#                   so a record may perfectly well be written for a server-half failure. The guard
#                   could only see `Tests/`, so the first such record would have been counted zero
#                   times and failed the suite with a message blaming the record for a blindness in
#                   the scan.
#   >>> sites       how many times that `source` appears across those trees, asserted exactly, and
#                   never zero. The `source` check alone only asks whether the text survives
#                   *somewhere*, and most records below have more than one site — so rewording one
#                   of six copies left the suite green while that copy's failures went back to
#                   counting as kills, measured by rewording one (PR #112 review, F4). A count is
#                   what makes the reword visible. When this number changes, the change itself is
#                   the question: a new test using the same helper is fine and the number goes up; a
#                   site that stopped matching is a signature that stopped protecting, and needs its
#                   own record or its own fix.
#                   **Zero is refused on its own terms** (SONNY-334). `found == sites` is satisfied
#                   by a scan that finds nothing when a record declares nothing, so zeroing the
#                   count was a way to make any failure of that check go green while leaving a
#                   record whose source protects nothing — the exact defect the count was added for,
#                   through the one door it did not watch. A source with no site left has stopped
#                   protecting anything and belongs deleted, not zeroed.
#   >>> reason      one line: why a failure like this says nothing about a mutant.
#
# What belongs here: a hang backstop, a wall-clock ceiling, anything whose failure is a statement
# about the machine. What does not: an ordinary assertion that happens to be failing today. This is
# not a quarantine for broken tests — a red test that is genuinely red still has to be fixed, and
# nothing here stops it failing the suite.

>>> signature This is main-actor starvation and not a stuck loop
>>> source This is main-actor starvation and not a stuck loop
>>> sites 1
>>> reason HangBackstop's own name for a wait that ran out of wall clock without ever getting enough turns on the shared main actor to observe anything (SONNY-302). It is a measurement of the actor's queue depth and says nothing whatever about the code under test, which is what the message itself tells a reader. Declared on the construct rather than on having watched a battery lose to it: the construct is new, and the failure it replaces was the single largest source of untrusted red in this suite.

>>> signature This deadline is a deadlock backstop, not a timing assertion
>>> source This deadline is a deadlock backstop, not a timing assertion
>>> sites 2
>>> reason A poll that ran out of wall clock, in HangBackstop.wait — which VisionSessionRunTests' waitUntil now forwards to — and in AgentActionExecutorTests' waitForLaunchSignal. Since SONNY-302 the first of those fires only after the wait has evaluated its condition at least 500 times, which is far stronger evidence than the bare clock it replaced, and the question of whether that makes it trustworthy was measured rather than assumed. It does not, and the reason is a cascade: a wait that gives up leaves its test running against a precondition that never arrived, and every later wait in that test then times out at a perfectly healthy six-millisecond cadence, which reaches the floor easily. Measured on this branch with 6b72909 checked out plus a counting-only probe patch, in the fourth of five instrumented runs: 19 of 19 timeouts that had collected a thousand observations or more were preceded, inside the same test, by one that had collected two or fewer. So load can still reach this wording, one step removed, and understating coverage stays the safe direction. The reach is 65 call-site lines among 77 test functions in that one file (grep -cE 'waitUntil[(]' and grep -c '^    @Test' over Tests/MacAgentTests/VisionSessionRunTests.swift on this branch; the patterns are bracketed and anchored so that prose about them is not counted as a call site), plus the one waitForLaunchSignal site.

>>> signature did not become idle before timeout
>>> source did not become idle before timeout
>>> sites 6
>>> reason The view-model hang backstop, in the six suites that word it this way. Same construct as above and the same 30 s deadline; not observed firing in the run that produced the note above, because those suites happened not to be the ones that lost. Declared on the construct, not on having watched it.

>>> signature did not settle before timeout
>>> source did not settle before timeout
>>> sites 2
>>> reason The same view-model hang backstop, in the two suites that word it "settle" instead of "become idle". Two wordings of one sentence is a DRY problem rather than a second construct, and SONNY-261 is where that is written down.

>>> signature the run never finished
>>> source the run never finished
>>> sites 1
>>> reason MemoryCommandCenterTests' own copy of the view-model hang backstop, which asserts its deadline inline rather than recording an issue. Raised to 2 by SONNY-136 when BackendOutageTests hand-rolled the same loop, and back to 1 when PR #153's F1 replaced that loop with HangBackstop.waitOrAbandon — which is the better answer for the reason F1 gives: a wall-clock loop in this process gets one or two looks in thirty seconds, so its deadline was measuring the main actor's queue depth and nothing else, and it failed the flagged suite on an unmodified tree in three of six runs. This site remains because MemoryCommandCenterTests is not this ticket's to change.

>>> signature was abandoned, so this test stopped here rather than asserting against a precondition that never arrived
>>> source was abandoned, so this test stopped here rather than asserting against a precondition that never arrived
>>> sites 1
>>> reason HangBackstop.waitOrAbandon's own error, thrown after the wait above it has already recorded whether it was stuck or starved (SONNY-136, PR #153's F2). Declared for the same reason as the two backstop wordings it rides behind, and it is the half that makes those two worth having: wait() records and returns, so the test body used to run on against a precondition that never arrived and record ordinary Expectation-failed assertions no declaration covers — a starved run indistinguishable from a mutation kill, demonstrated coming back red twice on a mutant neither failing test touches. Throwing makes the set of issues such a test can record finite, and this is the second and last member of it. A genuinely stuck wait is excused with it, which is the same trade the deadlock-backstop signature already makes and for the same reason: the cascade that follows a give-up is what destroys the evidence, whichever verdict caused it.

>>> signature Expectation failed: (elapsed →
>>> source #expect(elapsed < .seconds(
>>> sites 2
>>> reason A wall-clock ceiling. It measures the machine, not the code: the redaction ceiling was measured at 1465 ms idle and 11742 ms with a cold build alongside, at 94afca1, against a bound that was 5000 ms at the time. The ceiling is worth keeping as a tripwire for a pathological regression; its loss under load is worth nothing as evidence about a mutant.

>>> signature This is a server-suite hang backstop, not a timing assertion
>>> source This is a server-suite hang backstop, not a timing assertion
>>> sites 1
>>> reason The first server-half record, and the first whose source lives under server/test/ rather than Tests/ — which the guard could not see until SONNY-334 (SONNY-335, SONNY-241). It is the bound every test in a server/test/*.db.test.ts file waits under, replacing vitest's default 5000 ms testTimeout, which nobody chose for them. It covered two tests in auth.db.test.ts until SONNY-354 widened it to the whole database population — 303 tests across 13 files, and since PR #172 cycle 2 all 42 of their hook call sites too, through matching wrappers whose failure carries this same signature (grep -hcE '^ *itUnderHangBackstop[(]' server/test/*.db.test.ts | paste -sd+ - | bc answering 303, the same with it[(] answering 0, the four wrapper names answering 42 and the six raw hook names answering 0, all at 9df5d27) — enforced by backstop.test.ts, which fails the suite on a bare it( in one of those files and refuses a population of zero on its own terms. The line is drawn from measurement rather than taste: a database test waits on work in another process whose cost this one does not control, and the thinnest margin measured in that population was migrate.db.test.ts's rollback chain at 2483 ms of the 5000 ms default, a factor of two, while the non-database files run in-process at under 300 ms a file and keep vitest's default for that reason. Sixty seconds is 24x the worst those tests have been measured at under deliberate load (2527 ms at a one-minute load average of 155, with 40 CPU-bound node processes running) and 12x what the reported failures met, so a run that reaches it never finished rather than being slow — but a wall clock on work another process owns is a statement about the machine whatever the margin, which is what this file exists for, so it is declared. The Swift half's observation floor was tried here and rejected on measurement rather than on taste: at four load levels up to a load average of 194 the event loop ticked at 167-178 per second against a nominal 200, including the run where one of these tests took ten times its unloaded cost, so a floor set below the healthy population would have called that run a deadlock. What this record costs is now the whole database population's worth rather than two tests': a mutant that genuinely hangs one of them and breaks nothing else comes back UNATTRIBUTED rather than KILLED, which is true, and which exits 2 like a survivor rather than passing as a kill. What it does not cost is a mutant that makes one of them wrong — underHangBackstop rethrows the body's own error untouched, so an assertion failure carries none of this wording, and backstop.test.ts asserts the thrown object is identical rather than merely equal. The alternative SONNY-354 was filed proposing, and rejected: declaring vitest's own timed-out-in-N-ms wording. That message is shared by every test in this repository, so declaring it would excuse a genuine mutant-induced hang anywhere — and a mutant that makes a query never return is caught by a timeout and by nothing else, which is the one kill a battery most needs to count.
